    summary: 'The runtime API is the higher-level C interface that most applications and PyTorch use: `cudaMalloc`, `cudaMemcpyAsync`, `cudaStreamCreate`, `cudaEventRecord` and the `kernel<<<grid, block, smem, stream>>>(args)` launch syntax that `nvcc` lowers to `cudaLaunchKernel`. It links as `libcudart` into the application, initializes the device context lazily on first use, and is implemented on top of the lower-level driver API in `libcuda.so`, which the driver package installs.',
    purpose: 'Gives applications a convenient, mostly implicit-context interface to device memory, streams, events and kernel launches without managing modules and contexts by hand.',
    mechanics: 'Every runtime call first ensures the calling thread has a current context, creating or attaching to the device’s primary context on first use (this is the multi-hundred-millisecond first-call cost seen as `torch.cuda.init`). Memory calls (`cudaMalloc`, `cudaFree`) are synchronous and expensive (tens of microseconds to milliseconds) and can implicitly synchronize the device, which is why frameworks wrap them in a caching allocator. Copies come in synchronous (`cudaMemcpy`) and asynchronous (`cudaMemcpyAsync`) forms; asynchronous copies are only truly asynchronous from pinned host memory and on a non-default stream. Launches are asynchronous: `cudaLaunchKernel` validates arguments, packs them and enqueues the launch on the stream, then returns in a few microseconds; the GPU executes later. Errors follow two paths: a call returns an error for problems detected at the API (invalid argument, out of memory), while kernel-side faults (illegal address, misaligned access) are reported asynchronously by a later call and are sticky, meaning the context is unusable after them and the process must exit or re-create it. `cudaGetLastError` reads and clears the last launch error; `cudaPeekAtLastError` reads without clearing. `CUDA_LAUNCH_BLOCKING=1` forces every launch to synchronize, useful for attributing an asynchronous error to its kernel and useless for timing. The runtime is versioned with the toolkit; it requires a driver at least as new as its minimum, with forward-compatibility packages covering some older-driver cases.',
