    summary: 'The driver API is the lower-level interface implemented in `libcuda.so`, the user-mode component of the NVIDIA driver, versioned with the driver rather than the toolkit. It exposes explicit contexts, modules (loaded PTX or cubin), functions, memory and streams; the runtime API is a layer above it that hides contexts and modules. The user-mode driver is also where GPU commands are written into command buffers and where privileged operations are turned into `ioctl` calls to the kernel-mode driver.',
    purpose: 'Provides the explicit, stable ABI between applications (and the runtime) and the installed driver, and implements the host side of command submission, memory mapping and context management.',
    mechanics: 'Applications begin with `cuInit`, enumerate devices, and either create a context (`cuCtxCreate`) or retain the device’s primary context (`cuDevicePrimaryCtxRetain`, which the runtime uses). Code is loaded as modules from PTX (JIT-compiled by the driver) or from cubin/fatbin, and kernels are obtained as `CUfunction` handles launched with `cuLaunchKernel(f, grid, block, smem, stream, args, extra)`. Memory management includes `cuMemAlloc`/`cuMemFree` and the virtual memory management API (`cuMemCreate`, `cuMemMap`, `cuMemSetAccess`) that reserves address ranges and maps physical chunks separately, which is what PyTorch’s expandable segments build on. Operations that need the kernel-mode driver (allocating physical GPU memory, mapping it into the GPU page tables, creating channels, registering pinned host pages, waiting on synchronization primitives that block) are commonly issued as `ioctl`s on `/dev/nvidiactl` and `/dev/nvidia<N>`; launches and most stream work are not, they are written to user-mode command buffers and signalled through a doorbell register mapped into the process. The runtime binds to the driver API dynamically, so a toolkit release lists a minimum driver version; a newer driver runs older toolkits, and the forward-compatibility package lets some newer toolkits run on older data-center drivers. PyTorch uses the runtime for nearly everything and reaches for the driver API for specific features (virtual memory management, some IPC and graph functionality, `cuda-python` bindings).',
