    summary: 'A context is the GPU-side analogue of a process: it owns a virtual address space on the device, the loaded modules, streams, events and allocations, and a set of hardware channels used to submit work. Each process gets one primary context per device by default, created lazily on first use and costing hundreds of milliseconds and a few hundred megabytes of device memory. The GPU time-slices between contexts of different processes, with a context switch that saves and restores execution state, whereas MPS lets multiple processes share one context so their kernels can run concurrently.',
    purpose: 'Isolates address spaces and resources between processes on the same GPU and provides the container into which everything a process does on the device is scoped.',
    mechanics: 'Creating a context allocates GPU page tables for the process’s device address space, loads the module images the process needs (eagerly or lazily), creates command channels with the kernel driver, and reserves device memory for the driver’s own state; `nvidia-smi` shows this as a few hundred megabytes used before any tensor is allocated. Each host thread has a current context; the runtime API sets it to the primary context of the current device automatically, and `cudaSetDevice` switches it. Kernels of different contexts do not run concurrently on the same GPU by default: the hardware scheduler time-slices between them at a granularity of a few milliseconds (configurable on some data-center GPUs), and a switch saves and restores registers, shared memory and pipeline state, so two processes sharing a GPU each see reduced throughput plus switch overhead. MPS (Multi-Process Service) interposes a server that owns a single context; client processes submit work through it, so kernels from different clients can execute simultaneously, at the cost of weaker fault isolation (a fatal error can affect all clients) and optional per-client SM limits. MIG is different again: it partitions the hardware, and each partition then holds its own contexts. Contexts are destroyed at process exit; a sticky error makes the context unusable, and the usual recovery is to end the process. `CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES` controls which devices a process can create contexts on and renumbers them from zero.',
