    mechanics: 'Operations enqueued on one stream execute in order; operations on different streams have no ordering unless an event links them: `cudaEventRecord(e, s1)` then `cudaStreamWaitEvent(s2, e)` makes everything later in `s2` wait for everything earlier in `s1`, without blocking the host. The legacy default stream (stream 0) synchronizes with all other blocking streams on the same device, which serializes work unless streams are created with `cudaStreamNonBlocking` or the program is compiled for a per-thread default stream. Overlap is real only when the hardware has the resources: copy engines run copies concurrently with kernels, and kernels from different streams run concurrently only if the first does not fill the GPU. Stream priorities let the scheduler prefer blocks from a high-priority stream when both have pending work, which inference engines use to keep a latency-critical decode ahead of a bulk prefill or a KV transfer. Events with timing enabled give device timestamps (`cudaEventElapsedTime`) with sub-microsecond resolution; recording them costs little, but timing events disable some cross-stream optimizations. Host callbacks (`cudaLaunchHostFunc`) run a host function when the stream reaches that point, without making CUDA calls inside it. PyTorch’s caching allocator tags each block with the stream it was allocated on; using a tensor on another stream requires `record_stream` or an explicit event so the block is not freed and reused while the other stream is still reading it.',
