    summary: 'A kernel launch is a host-side operation of a few microseconds that packs the kernel handle, grid and block dimensions, shared-memory size and argument bytes into a command in a user-mode command buffer (pushbuffer) belonging to the stream’s channel, then writes a doorbell register mapped into the process to tell the GPU’s command processor that work is available. No system call is made per launch; the kernel-mode driver was involved only when the channel and buffers were set up. The GPU dequeues commands asynchronously, so the host can run ahead by many launches, and the launch-bound regime begins when the host cannot issue launches as fast as the GPU retires them.',
    purpose: 'Delivers work to the GPU with minimal host involvement, so that the CPU can queue thousands of kernels ahead and the device is never waiting on a host round trip per kernel.',
    mechanics: 'On the host, `cudaLaunchKernel` validates the configuration against the function’s resource limits, copies the argument buffer, and appends a launch command to the stream’s pushbuffer in pinned, GPU-visible host memory; the total is commonly 3-5 µs of CPU time in a tight loop, more with profilers or many arguments. The doorbell write is a store to a memory-mapped register; the command processor (host interface plus front end) reads the pushbuffer over PCIe or through its own DMA, decodes the launch, and hands the grid to the work distributor, which assigns thread blocks to SMs. Device-side launch latency (from doorbell to the first block executing when the GPU is idle) is of the order of a few microseconds; when the queue is non-empty the next kernel starts as soon as dependencies and resources allow, hiding that latency. The queue has finite depth: after roughly a thousand outstanding launches (implementation dependent) the host call blocks until space frees, which is the mechanism by which an unsynchronized loop eventually throttles. A kernel that runs for 5 µs launched at 5 µs intervals keeps the GPU exactly busy; anything shorter leaves gaps, and a decode step made of hundreds of such kernels becomes launch-bound: wall time equals host issue time rather than GPU time. Remedies in order of cost: remove Python and dispatcher overhead per launch (compiled paths), fuse kernels so there are fewer launches, or capture the sequence as a CUDA graph and replay it with a single launch whose device-side execution walks the dependency graph without host involvement.',
