    summary: 'Allocating device memory through the driver is slow and can synchronize the whole device, so PyTorch keeps freed blocks in pools and hands them out again without touching the driver. The device therefore shows memory as reserved (held by the allocator) that is larger than what tensors currently occupy (allocated); the difference is cache plus fragmentation. Inference engines take this a step further and preallocate the entire KV cache as one pool at startup so no allocation of any kind happens in the decode loop.',
    purpose: 'Makes tensor allocation cheap and deterministic in the hot path and prevents implicit device synchronization from `cudaFree`.',
    mechanics: 'Requests are rounded to multiples of 512 bytes and served from two pools: small (blocks under 1 MB, carved from 2 MB segments) and large (segments of at least 20 MB or the rounded request size). A request first searches the pool for a free block of adequate size on the same stream; if none exists, the allocator calls `cudaMalloc` for a new segment, and if that fails it frees all cached unused segments and retries before raising an out-of-memory error. Freed blocks return to the pool immediately (they are not released to the driver) and adjacent free blocks are coalesced within a segment; blocks are tagged with the allocating stream so reuse never races an in-flight kernel on another stream. Fragmentation arises when many segments each hold a few live blocks: `memory_reserved` stays high while `memory_allocated` is low, and a large request fails even though the total free memory would suffice. `max_split_size_mb` limits how large blocks are split to reduce this; `expandable_segments:True` uses the driver’s virtual memory management API (`cuMemCreate`/`cuMemMap`) to grow segments in place so that a pool behaves like one large contiguous region that can extend, removing most fragmentation for varying shapes. `torch.cuda.empty_cache()` releases unused cached segments to the driver, which helps other processes on the same GPU and hurts the calling process by reintroducing `cudaMalloc` calls. vLLM-style engines instead compute `free memory × gpu_memory_utilization − weights − profiled activation peak`, allocate that as a fixed set of KV blocks once, and manage the blocks themselves with a block table, which makes memory behaviour independent of request shape and eliminates OOM at run time in exchange for a hard admission limit.',
    input: 'Allocation and free requests from tensor operations with size, dtype, device and stream.',
    output: 'Device pointers into cached segments; occasional cudaMalloc/cudaFree calls to the driver.',
    tensorShape: 'Segments of 2 MB (small pool) or ≥ 20 MB (large pool), split into blocks rounded to 512 bytes; expandable segments grow in 2 MB physical chunks (defaults, version dependent).',
    memory: 'Reserved ≥ allocated ≥ live tensor bytes; the gap is cache and fragmentation. Peak reserved decides whether a workload fits, not average allocated.',
    compute: 'Cached allocation is sub-microsecond on the host; a cache miss that calls cudaMalloc costs tens of microseconds to milliseconds and may synchronize.',
    knobs: ['PYTORCH_CUDA_ALLOC_CONF: expandable_segments, max_split_size_mb, garbage_collection_threshold', 'Preallocated pools (KV cache, static buffers) vs dynamic allocation', 'empty_cache() policy when sharing a GPU', 'Custom allocator plug-in (CUDAPluggableAllocator)', 'Memory-utilization fraction in inference engines'],
