    summary: 'The GPU’s DMA engines can only read and write host memory that is page-locked (pinned), because the OS may otherwise move or swap the pages while the transfer is in flight. Copies from ordinary pageable memory therefore go through a driver-owned pinned staging buffer in chunks, which halves effective bandwidth and blocks the host thread. Unified (managed) memory presents one pointer usable from both sides and migrates pages on demand at fault granularity, convenient but slow when access patterns thrash. Mapped pinned memory lets kernels read host memory directly over PCIe without any copy at all.',
    purpose: 'Makes host-device transfers asynchronous and full-bandwidth, and offers convenience models (managed, mapped) for cases where explicit copies are impractical.',
    mechanics: '`cudaHostAlloc` or `cudaHostRegister` pins pages (the kernel driver locks them and records their physical addresses, commonly through an ioctl) and maps them into the GPU’s address space so the copy engine can DMA directly; `cudaMemcpyAsync` from such memory returns immediately and runs concurrently with kernels on other streams. From pageable memory the runtime copies chunks into an internal pinned buffer and DMAs from there, so the call cannot return until the source has been staged, throughput is commonly about half of pinned, and overlap with compute is limited. In PyTorch, `DataLoader(pin_memory=True)` and `tensor.pin_memory()` produce pinned tensors, and `.to(device, non_blocking=True)` is only asynchronous from them. Pinning has costs: pinned pages are unswappable and count against `ulimit -l` style limits and container memory, allocation is slow (milliseconds for large buffers), and pinning most of host RAM starves the OS. Managed memory (`cudaMallocManaged`) allocates pages that either the CPU or the GPU can touch; on Pascal and later a GPU access to a page resident on the host raises a fault, and the driver migrates the page (4 KB to 2 MB granularity) over PCIe, with each fault costing tens of microseconds, so a kernel that touches millions of non-resident pages can run orders of magnitude slower than with explicit copies. `cudaMemPrefetchAsync` and `cudaMemAdvise` move data ahead of use and set preferred locations to avoid faults; on NVLink-connected CPU-GPU systems (Grace Hopper, example) coherent access reduces the penalty. Mapped pinned memory (`cudaHostAllocMapped`) gives the kernel a device pointer to host memory; every access is a PCIe transaction at PCIe latency and bandwidth, suitable for small or streaming-once data such as flags and sampling outputs, not for weights.',
