    summary: 'A CUDA graph is a recorded DAG of kernels, copies and event dependencies that the runtime can launch as a single unit. Instead of the host issuing hundreds of launches per decode step at a few microseconds each, it issues one `cudaGraphLaunch` and the device walks the graph itself, so a launch-bound step becomes GPU-bound. The constraint is that everything in the graph is frozen at capture time: kernel arguments, tensor addresses, shapes and the stream topology, which is why inference engines capture one graph per batch-size bucket and run them on static input buffers.',
    purpose: 'Eliminates per-kernel host launch overhead and inter-kernel gaps for repeated, fixed-shape work such as the decode step of a serving engine.',
    mechanics: 'Capture is stream-based: `cudaStreamBeginCapture` puts a stream into capture mode, and every operation enqueued on it (and on streams joined to it through events) is recorded as a node instead of executed; `cudaStreamEndCapture` returns a `cudaGraph_t`, and `cudaGraphInstantiate` turns it into an executable graph with device-side resources allocated. Replay via `cudaGraphLaunch` enqueues the whole DAG in one call with a host cost of the order of tens of microseconds regardless of node count, and the device schedules nodes with dependencies resolved on the GPU, often with smaller gaps between kernels than separate launches achieve. Synchronizing calls, allocations from the default allocator and CPU-side data reads are illegal during capture, so PyTorch gives each captured graph a private memory pool from the caching allocator so that addresses are stable across replays, and requires inputs to be copied into static tensors before each replay. Since arguments are baked in, any change to shape, pointer, or control flow requires a different graph; engines capture a set of graphs for batch sizes such as 1, 2, 4, 8, ... up to a maximum during warm-up (seconds to minutes and hundreds of MB of memory), pad each decode batch up to the nearest captured size, and fall back to eager for larger or unusual shapes. Graph updates (`cudaGraphExecUpdate`) can change kernel parameters of an instantiated graph without re-instantiation when the topology is unchanged, which some engines use to adjust pointers. Attention over a paged KV cache is graph-compatible because block tables are read from device memory, whereas anything that depends on host-side sequence lengths must be expressed through device tensors. torch.compile’s `reduce-overhead` mode automates capture per compiled graph and shape.',
    input: 'A sequence of stream operations recorded during capture; static input and output buffers at replay.',
    output: 'An executable graph; on replay, the same kernels executing with one host launch.',
    tensorShape: 'A graph is a DAG of nodes (kernel, memcpy, memset, event, host, child graph); engines hold one executable graph per (batch size bucket, model configuration).',
    compute: 'Host cost per step drops from `N_kernels × ~3-5 µs` to roughly one launch; device time is unchanged or slightly lower from tighter scheduling.',
    memory: 'Private pool per graph pins the peak working set of the captured step; multiple graphs may share a pool when captured in sequence; capture adds hundreds of MB in typical engines.',
    knobs: ['Capture sizes (batch-size buckets) and maximum captured batch', 'Shared vs separate memory pools across graphs', 'Which ops are in the graph vs eager (sampling, prefill)', 'Warm-up policy for capture at startup', 'Graph update vs recapture on parameter change'],
